// `#[derive]` diagnostic codes.
//
// Written in subset 1 (meta/SUBSET_1.md §4). Cycle 0.6.5.
//
// ITS OWN PREFIX, not `MACRO-`. Derive emits declarations and runs in the expansion
// phase (D-123, "a macro that does not look like one"), but a reader filtering a
// log for derive failures is asking a different question from one filtering for
// expansion failures — and the codes are what they filter on.
//
// Centralised for the reason 0.1.6, 0.2.1, 0.3.1, 0.4.1, 0.5.1 and 0.6.1 give: a
// mistyped code literal at a call site silently invents a NEW code, and the test
// asserting the old one then fails for a reason unrelated to the bug it guards.

// A NAME THAT IS NOT ONE OF THE SEVEN. A typo, or a trait the program declared and
// expected the compiler to know how to generate.
mod:derive_codes;
pub func:DERIVE_UNKNOWN = string() never fails      { pass "NITPICK-DERIVE-001"; };

// A NAME THAT WAS DERIVABLE ONCE AND IS NOT. `Default` and `Display` were on
// `TRAITS_REFERENCE.md`'s list of nine and D-123 removed both, for reasons the
// message carries — a reader who wrote one of them is not making a typo and does
// not need to be told they might be.
pub func:DERIVE_REMOVED = string() never fails      { pass "NITPICK-DERIVE-002"; };

// THE SAME TRAIT DERIVED TWICE ON ONE DECLARATION. Two impls of one trait for one
// type, which is what `check_coherence` refuses when they are hand-written — caught
// here instead, because a derive that generated both would report at code the
// reader never wrote.
pub func:DERIVE_DUPLICATE = string() never fails    { pass "NITPICK-DERIVE-003"; };

// `#[derive]` ON SOMETHING THAT IS NOT A TYPE. Every one of the seven is generated
// FROM a type's shape — fields, or variants — so there is nothing to generate from
// on a function or a module.
pub func:DERIVE_TARGET = string() never fails       { pass "NITPICK-DERIVE-004"; };

// A `simd` FIELD REFUSES THE DERIVES THAT HAVE NO LANE MEANING (D-194,
// 1.3.1). A derived `Eq` works — lane inequality collapses with `.any()` —
// but an ORDER over a vector has no scalar verdict, a HASH has no impl to
// call, and a rendering has no `ToString`; generating any of them would
// point a type error at source nobody wrote. Write the impl by hand and
// say what a vector means for the type.
pub func:DERIVE_SIMD_FIELD = string() never fails   { pass "NITPICK-DERIVE-005"; };

// A MEMBER NO DERIVED BODY CAN BE WRITTEN FOR (D-250, 1.5.1b step 3b; widened
// to every derive by D-258, 1.5.2b step 3), refused at the USER'S declaration,
// naming the field or variant, its spelling and why: an OWNING or ERASED
// builtin (`buffer`, `dyn`, `List<…>`, `OwnedFd`, an arena, a sync primitive,
// a `Channel`, an `Optional`, a `Result`, `cstring`, `any`, a function type,
// `complex` over a parameter) is compared, hashed, rendered or copied by a
// rule only its author can choose; a `string` PAYLOAD cannot be bound by the
// `pick` a payload body needs without consuming it (D-216's is the only
// binding form); a pointer has identity and nothing else (`Eq` and `Clone`
// derive over one, the rest do not); an array has no `==`, order, hash or
// rendering, and copies only when its elements are scalars. The impl is
// written by hand and says what the comparison, hash, rendering or copy means.
pub func:DERIVE_UNCOMPARABLE = string() never fails { pass "NITPICK-DERIVE-006"; };
